> yesterday I saw a slack 7.1.0 machine whose uname -r output was=20
> 2.4.7-greased-turkey or something like that
This was a (bad) joke on the part of Mr. Torvalds, as this kernel
was released near an American holiday on which eating Turkey is
traditional. Thanksgiving, possibly.

> actually, kernels can have a name of their own given by the person who=20
> compiled it ?
Yep; look at the EXTRA_VERSION line at the top of the Makefile. In this
case, it should be set to ``-greased-turkey''.

